  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 725 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of the week with increasing chances for showers and storms \nby late week.\n\nEvening weather analysis showed strong upper ridging over the \nsouthern Plains with a weak shortwave passing by to our north, \nand sfc high pressure gradually shifting offshore. Additionally,\na large area of cloud cover was noted from SE VA/NE NC and\nextending offshore due to an inverted trough offshore. A few\nisolated showers were noted across southern portions of NE NC\nwith additional isolated showers approaching the NW/N portions\nof the FA. However, this convection is expected to weaken\nquickly with the loss of diurnal instability after sunset.\nOtherwise, partly cloudy skies continue through tonight with\nlows in the lower 70s for most (mid 70s along the coast and\nlocalized upper 60s possible).\n\nThe main story for the next few days will be warming \ntemperatures and potential Heat Advisories by Wednesday/Thursday.\nThe above mentioned strong upper level ridge will expand \neastward over the Ohio Valley and into the Mid- Atlantic early \nthis week. Warm advection and deep mixing will allow for \ntemperatures to rise into the low to mid 90s on Monday and \nTuesday (locally upper 90s on Tuesday). Max heat indices on \nMonday and Tuesday should generally average 100 to 104 due to \ndeep mixing keeping dewpoints in the upper 60s to around 70. \nSubsidence under the the upper ridge will cap most convective \ndevelopment, but an isolated aftn/evening storm is possible on \nthe MD Eastern Shore on both Monday and Tuesday. However, \nconfidence in this is very low.\n\nThe ridge peaks in strength Wednesday before flattening later in the \nweek. Wednesday will see high temperatures likely in the mid to \nupper 90s for many inland locations. The NBM continues to show 100F \nreadings across southwest portions of the area, but this is likely \ntoo warm and is a known bias. Still, Heat Advisories may be needed \nfor at least southern and southeastern portions of the area on \nWednesday, and potentially even the I-95 Corridor/Richmond Metro. \nHeights start to fall a bit on Thursday and especially on Friday as \nthe ridge gradually breaks down. An upper level trough is progged to \ntrack from the Great Lakes into New England late this week and push \na cold front toward the Mid-Atlantic region. Thursday may be the \nhottest day of the period due to heating ahead of the front and a \nslight increase in humidity. Highs on Thursday will range from the \nmid to upper 90s for most inland locations. Heat Advisories may be \nneeded for much of the area on Thursday. At this time, model \nconsensus shows the front crossing the area sometime from late \nFriday-Saturday morning as low pressure deepens near Nova Scotia. \nIsolated afternoon/evening storms are possible (mainly N of I-64) on \nThursday with storm chances expanding to the entire area on Friday \nas the front gets closer to the area.  AI/machine learning tools \ncontinue to hint at a severe weather potential on Thursday and \nFriday, but this will ultimately depend on convective coverage. \nGiven how hot it will be, there will likely be a localized damaging \nwind threat with any storms that do form. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures return over Labor Day weekend \nwith decreasing precipitation chances during the second half of the \nweekend.\n\nThe upper trough is progged to amplify off the Mid-Atlantic coast \nthis weekend as high pressure tries to build in from the north. This \nwill result in onshore flow, cooler temps, and decreasing precip \nchances through the weekend. There could still be a fair bit of \ncloud cover (especially near the coast) with the onshore flow. Highs \nwill generally be in the 80s with lower humidity.",
